WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.515 --> 00:00:06.550
Punching with your lance like that,

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:08.020
no. Not good.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.850
You're not going to get
anything by throwing a punch

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:13.010
except for a broken
wrist or a broken hand.

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:13.990
Hi, I'm Shane Adams,

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:15.260
the owner of the Knights of Valour

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:17.180
full-contact jousting company.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.499
I've been jousting for well over 25 years,

00:00:19.499 --> 00:00:20.970
so I have 20 international

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.810
jousting championships under my belt.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:24.593
Today we're going to be
looking at horseback fights

00:00:24.593 --> 00:00:27.434
in movies and judging how real they are.

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:35.140
They hit, but it was just a glancing blow.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:37.530
There is no penalty for
missing your opponent,

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:40.350
but you're going to be behind in points.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.899
If you hit the target, whether it be

00:00:41.899 --> 00:00:46.040
a smooth plate or whether
it be a targe shield,

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.580
you get a certain amount of points.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.560
And, ultimately, if you
unhorse your opponent,

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.430
in the full-contact style

00:00:52.430 --> 00:00:54.398
that's the maximum amount of points.

00:00:54.398 --> 00:00:58.787
There's probably well over 19, 20, 25

00:00:58.787 --> 00:01:01.313
different styles of jousting.

00:01:04.310 --> 00:01:07.410
Yeah. See that ridge around
that shield? Bad news.

00:01:07.410 --> 00:01:10.100
You don't want a lip around the shield.

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:12.360
You don't want any spot where that lance

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.920
is going to connect and lock into the lip.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.490
You want to be able to deflect the lance

00:01:16.490 --> 00:01:17.570
away from your body.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:19.903
So, completely wrong shield.

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:26.798
The jousting that was there,

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:30.550
it was decent for a
theatrical-style joust.

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:32.280
If they were true knights,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:34.120
they wouldn't be holding
their shields like that.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:36.320
They were leaving themselves
completely exposed

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:39.110
from the top of the
shield to throat, head.

00:01:39.110 --> 00:01:41.350
That scene where the horse actually

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:43.540
does fall into the joust list,

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:46.230
usually it would be the opposite way.

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.251
So the person would be getting hit

00:01:48.251 --> 00:01:49.851
coming off to the right side.

00:01:49.851 --> 00:01:52.817
Based on the fact that we do
real full-contact jousting,

00:01:52.817 --> 00:01:54.130
we always drop the reins.

00:01:54.130 --> 00:01:56.220
So that way there, if you get off balance,

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:58.415
you're not going to be pulling
your horse to the extent

00:01:58.415 --> 00:01:59.780
that you're going to be pulling your horse

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:01.390
down onto the ground with you.

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:03.360
I'm 265 pounds.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.660
Add my armor, I'm 415 pounds,

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.933
riding a 2,000-pound horse

00:02:08.933 --> 00:02:11.891
charging at 20, 25 miles an hour

00:02:11.891 --> 00:02:14.566
against the same opposing force.

00:02:14.566 --> 00:02:15.580
It's a car wreck.

00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:19.239
So when we hit, there is a
lot of force being generated.

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:24.595
Both guys were not
wearing proper equipment

00:02:24.595 --> 00:02:26.745
as far as gorgets to protect their necks,

00:02:26.745 --> 00:02:27.828
because the lance can hit

00:02:27.828 --> 00:02:29.810
and come up and straight into your throat.

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:31.818
You could tell right away,

00:02:31.818 --> 00:02:33.893
looking at the tips of the lances,

00:02:33.893 --> 00:02:36.760
the Hound's lance didn't
have a coronel on it.

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.790
Didn't have, like, a tip on
the end. It was a sheer point.

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:41.860
Knights back in the olden days,

00:02:41.860 --> 00:02:43.890
the nobility were the ones jousting.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:45.790
They didn't want to go out
there and kill each other.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:50.090
And the ends of the lances
had coronels put on them,

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:53.660
again, to disperse the impact
force on the knight's armor.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.870
It's like a cast fist put on.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:58.070
Well, if you don't do that,

00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:00.810
you have a scene possibly like this.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:04.330
And historically that could
have definitely happened.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:08.360
So, I'm a total "Game of Thrones"
geek, and I love the show.

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:11.221
However, have to give it a 4.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:19.000
They utilized just that connection

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:20.440
with the horse without the stirrup.

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:21.800
The stirrup didn't really come into play

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:24.970
until the battle of Hastings in 1066.

00:03:24.970 --> 00:03:28.390
A Roman cavalry, it's great riders,

00:03:28.390 --> 00:03:30.195
because they're basically riding

00:03:30.195 --> 00:03:32.386
just with that feel and
connection underneath them.

00:03:32.386 --> 00:03:36.661
Where the Percherons are
now 17, 18, 19 hands tall,

00:03:36.661 --> 00:03:38.280
they would've never been that tall.

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:39.896
They were a lot shorter back then.

00:03:39.896 --> 00:03:42.170
As a matter of fact, they were more

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:45.086
almost the size of a large
pony, to be honest with you.

00:03:45.086 --> 00:03:46.570
14 to 15 hands.

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.030
His horse was tripped up.

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:54.410
Down the horse went, and down you go.

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:58.830
How does it feel to
come off of your horse?

00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:00.750
Well, flying through
the air is pretty easy.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.640
It's the sudden stop
when you hit the ground

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:04.640
that really sucks, just
to be honest with you.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.360
He rolled, so he was fine.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.680
It's muddy terrain.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.430
There's been times when
I've come off my horse

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:14.650
that I've landed on my
feet next to the horse

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:16.780
holding onto the reins.
Stay on your horse.

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:19.097
But if your horse is going to go down,

00:04:19.097 --> 00:04:21.577
it's best for you to
get clear of your horse,

00:04:21.577 --> 00:04:26.577
'cause you don't want that
1,500- to 2,000-pound horse

00:04:27.265 --> 00:04:28.750
body-slamming on top of you.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:32.440
In my jousting career, I
have very little injuries

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:34.120
from my full-contact jousting career.

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:36.151
The more injuries I've had

00:04:36.151 --> 00:04:40.085
are training horses for
the sport of jousting.

00:04:40.085 --> 00:04:41.834
You're getting on a horse

00:04:41.834 --> 00:04:44.834
in an outfit from "Battlestar Galactica."

00:04:44.834 --> 00:04:45.840
Cylon, you know what I mean?

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:47.640
Like, that's going to freak a horse out.

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.165
I'd give the whole scene a definite 8.

00:04:59.671 --> 00:05:03.010
So, you have two different
styles of jousting.

00:05:03.010 --> 00:05:07.330
You have Heath Ledger,
with a targe shield,

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:10.580
and you have his competitor coming down

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:12.744
in full-plate harness,

00:05:12.744 --> 00:05:17.365
more of a northern Italian
style, where he had no shield.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:21.839
Get it in the cradle,
get it in the cradle.

00:05:21.839 --> 00:05:23.320
Get it in the cradle!

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:26.070
Where they were saying,
"Get it in the cradle,

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:29.780
get it in the cradle," before
the cradle was the arret.

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:32.021
And on the ends of the lances,

00:05:32.021 --> 00:05:34.805
they'd have a small disk of wood

00:05:34.805 --> 00:05:38.237
or a ring set in place

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:40.230
to be able to catch this arret.

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:42.640
So when you bring that lance down,

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:45.885
that lance will slide in
and lock into that arret.

00:05:45.885 --> 00:05:49.320
And so it'll stop the lance
from going any farther back.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:51.680
So all that pressure comes onto your body,

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:54.680
and you're able to give that
much more of a forceful hit

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:58.130
with your body to be able
to break those lances

00:05:58.130 --> 00:05:59.910
and ultimately unhorse your opponents.

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:02.420
Try and hold on to 1,800 pounds of force.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:04.386
I hate to admit this,

00:06:04.386 --> 00:06:07.535
but I broke my hands seven times

00:06:07.535 --> 00:06:11.530
trying to learn how to
give devastating hits.

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:12.680
And I don't know how many times

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:15.132
that a bone has been
sticking through my thumb

00:06:15.132 --> 00:06:16.980
in an actual jousting competition.

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:19.348
And even in this movie,
you see these horses

00:06:19.348 --> 00:06:21.790
completely decked out, but it's deceiving.

00:06:21.790 --> 00:06:23.040
So, a lot of people would think,

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.530
"Oh, they're wearing that
armor to protect the horse."

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:27.630
Well, the horse was never a target.

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:30.610
As a matter of fact, back
in that period of time,

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:32.470
if you struck your opponent's horse,

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:33.910
you'd be stripped of land and title.

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:35.010
Why'd they have armor?

00:06:35.010 --> 00:06:37.920
Well, it was more of a car show.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.800
They didn't want to
bring their Volkswagen.

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:41.345
They brought their Ferrari.

00:06:41.345 --> 00:06:42.703
"Hey, check me out!"

00:06:42.703 --> 00:06:45.978
And that's what that horse armor was for.

00:06:49.670 --> 00:06:51.150
Remove your helmet.

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:52.870
William: I'm afraid the
final blow of the lance

00:06:52.870 --> 00:06:54.350
has bent it onto my head.

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:58.510
Where it smashed in his helmet
and he can't get it off,

00:06:58.510 --> 00:06:59.875
it still happened back then

00:06:59.875 --> 00:07:02.180
and still happens today's
modern time as well.

00:07:02.180 --> 00:07:05.050
One of my dear friends
took a shot in the helmet

00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:08.530
and spun his entire helmet
around, breaking his nose.

00:07:08.530 --> 00:07:11.127
So it looked like,

00:07:11.127 --> 00:07:14.110
literally it spun his head like a top.

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:16.350
Of course we're having
problems getting it off

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:18.870
'cause his whole face was
all smushed up in there.

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:21.155
Even if the target's down here,

00:07:21.155 --> 00:07:23.410
often you do get hit in the head.

00:07:23.410 --> 00:07:24.900
Of course, Heath Ledger's helmet,

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:26.800
no person in their right mind

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:28.650
would've worn that helmet.

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:33.480
You do not want the lance
to get in towards your face.

00:07:33.480 --> 00:07:36.620
This helmet, you'll see a
lot of different designs.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:38.390
This is called a sparrow's beak helm.

00:07:38.390 --> 00:07:42.580
It's there because when you
receive a direct lance hit,

00:07:42.580 --> 00:07:44.330
you don't want a flat surface,

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:47.290
like Heath Ledger received in that hit

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:48.800
where it caved his helmet in.

00:07:48.800 --> 00:07:53.670
It's very, very difficult to
cave this style of helmet in,

00:07:53.670 --> 00:07:57.340
and that's why I use it
for my jousting career.

00:07:57.340 --> 00:08:00.071
Full contact is probably

00:08:00.071 --> 00:08:03.360
one of the stupidest
thing a man could ever do.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.945
I'd give it a 7 1/2.

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:16.070
The goblins open up the
door, and they charge down.

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:19.100
That's a true maneuver.

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:20.449
That can easily happen,

00:08:20.449 --> 00:08:23.733
and them knocking down because
they caught them at surprise.

00:08:23.733 --> 00:08:27.602
You take 10 guys and put
them behind each other

00:08:27.602 --> 00:08:29.584
and take speed of horse,

00:08:29.584 --> 00:08:31.300
that 2,000 pounds

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.857
or 1,500 pounds for a lighter horse

00:08:33.857 --> 00:08:35.449
charging into that,

00:08:35.449 --> 00:08:38.230
it's a bowling ball knocking
down bowling ball pins.

00:08:38.230 --> 00:08:39.275
So the question is,

00:08:39.275 --> 00:08:41.950
can you keep up that
inertia and keep on going?

00:08:41.950 --> 00:08:45.232
So, the initial knockdown will
knock the wind out of you.

00:08:45.232 --> 00:08:46.065
It might not kill you,

00:08:46.065 --> 00:08:49.848
but it's the other 50, 100, 1,000,

00:08:49.848 --> 00:08:53.470
2,000, 3,000 horses
trampling over top of you

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:56.470
just like cutting grass
on a Sunday afternoon.

00:08:56.470 --> 00:08:58.630
You're going to mow down
what's in front of you.

00:08:58.630 --> 00:09:01.840
So, very true and could easily happen.

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:06.153
And it did happen
historically. Absolutely.

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:10.870
Do you see King Théoden

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:13.320
kind of wobbling off balance a little bit?

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.010
It could be the fact that the actor

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:17.093
wasn't comfortable in the saddle.

00:09:17.093 --> 00:09:19.552
Picking up a weapon and
wielding it on horseback,

00:09:19.552 --> 00:09:22.900
again, is that much more
attention to the weapon

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:25.730
and less attention on what
you're actually riding.

00:09:25.730 --> 00:09:28.600
When I train people how to joust,

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:30.425
I always tell them,

00:09:30.425 --> 00:09:32.833
from the hips down rides the horse,

00:09:32.833 --> 00:09:35.990
from the hips up does the task.

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:39.520
So the upper body can stay glued

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:42.360
and have a good platform to
be able to wield that sword

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:45.180
or couch that lance or spear that ring

00:09:45.180 --> 00:09:49.014
or whatever your job entails
on that given specific day.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:09:59.750
All the goblins this
time prepped and prepared

00:09:59.750 --> 00:10:02.200
with the long pole arms

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.423
locked down and locked in place.

00:10:04.423 --> 00:10:06.620
It's a truly historical defense

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:08.433
against the heavy horse charge,

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:12.750
to the extent that they actually stopped,

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:14.768
a lot of the knights would stop

00:10:14.768 --> 00:10:16.155
doing that heavy horse charge

00:10:16.155 --> 00:10:19.240
because that horse was
worth so much to them.

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:20.640
If they actually had to fight,

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:22.239
sometimes they'd dismount and run in

00:10:22.239 --> 00:10:24.570
and fight on foot and
not take their horse.

00:10:24.570 --> 00:10:27.070
You can train a horse all your life

00:10:27.070 --> 00:10:28.750
to do this one specific job,

00:10:28.750 --> 00:10:31.690
and all of a sudden it's go time,

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:33.793
and the horse realizes it's really stupid

00:10:33.793 --> 00:10:35.563
and he'd rather you just
go and fight yourself

00:10:35.563 --> 00:10:38.663
and he's going to go back and
hang out with the sumpter.

00:10:38.663 --> 00:10:41.350
They had a lot of good
historical elements in there

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:43.395
and a lot of good action

00:10:43.395 --> 00:10:45.410
that could have been
seen on a battlefield.

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:47.827
So I actually give it a 7.

00:10:49.144 --> 00:10:50.877
[gunshots]

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:53.800
So, in this situation,

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:57.620
a loud bang would just
make the horse run faster.

00:10:57.620 --> 00:10:59.460
It's not going to know
where that's coming from.

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:01.610
It would shy more of the motorcycles

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:04.610
coming up beside it than
it would anything else.

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:07.388
It would not want to be
near the motorcycles.

00:11:07.388 --> 00:11:09.837
But again, in that scenario,

00:11:09.837 --> 00:11:11.503
where you have cars on left and right,

00:11:11.503 --> 00:11:14.060
the only way it really can go is forward.

00:11:14.060 --> 00:11:16.550
So that horse is steering
himself at that point.

00:11:16.550 --> 00:11:18.320
If you have a superfast thoroughbred,

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:21.050
you're talking, down a street like this,

00:11:21.050 --> 00:11:22.875
easily 35 miles an hour.

00:11:30.518 --> 00:11:32.578
[laughs] Great scene.

00:11:32.578 --> 00:11:35.120
It looks like Keanu Reeves did
all of his own stunt there.

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:38.203
You have to be a great
rider to be able to do that,

00:11:38.203 --> 00:11:39.660
and he pulled it off.

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:42.030
You know, you could tell
he had a stunt saddle on.

00:11:42.030 --> 00:11:45.427
It had a really high pommel
that he was able to grab

00:11:45.427 --> 00:11:46.552
when he put his foot in the stirrup

00:11:46.552 --> 00:11:47.810
to go to the side of the horse

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:52.102
to be able to shoot from the
front barrel of the chest.

00:11:52.102 --> 00:11:55.930
In real life, you wouldn't
have a pommel that high

00:11:55.930 --> 00:11:57.550
in front of you to be able to grab.

00:11:57.550 --> 00:11:59.616
You would find that for stunt riders.

00:11:59.616 --> 00:12:02.791
Not to say that a good equestrian can't,

00:12:02.791 --> 00:12:05.760
just on a Western saddle,
be able to do that.

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:08.680
It was a really good
stunt, very realistic,

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:10.880
and could easily happen.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:13.733
I'd probably give it an 8.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:22.020
This whole scene is absolutely fantastic.

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:24.950
Back then they didn't have
a green screen or just CGI.

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:27.640
They knocked it out of the park.

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:32.330
That is 100% what I believe, historically,

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:35.080
a chariot race would've been like.

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:37.890
A lot of people died in the chariot races.

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:39.362
It was a very dangerous thing.

00:12:39.362 --> 00:12:40.920
I drive a chariot.

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:43.607
The more horses you add,

00:12:43.607 --> 00:12:45.710
the more power and more dangerous it gets.

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:47.250
The chariots back in that time

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:51.510
were pulled by one,
two, three, four horses.

00:12:51.510 --> 00:12:55.143
Of course, you see all these
cars doing drifting on tracks.

00:12:55.143 --> 00:12:56.590
Well, you know, that's nothing,

00:12:56.590 --> 00:12:59.848
because we can do drifting
with a horse and a chariot.

00:12:59.848 --> 00:13:01.092
It's amazing.

00:13:05.920 --> 00:13:08.340
Those horses are connected by reins

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:11.260
from the lead horses all the way back

00:13:11.260 --> 00:13:12.970
to the driver sitting in the chariot.

00:13:12.970 --> 00:13:15.790
In the rider's hands, in this point,

00:13:15.790 --> 00:13:19.421
he would have a set for the left horses

00:13:19.421 --> 00:13:20.780
and a set for the right horses,

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:22.370
being able to control that.

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:24.386
Some riders would have

00:13:24.386 --> 00:13:27.100
every horse reins coming into his hands.

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:30.003
And there is so much power and force

00:13:30.003 --> 00:13:33.410
that those drivers, to get
those chariots to stop,

00:13:33.410 --> 00:13:35.210
'cause there really was no brakes,

00:13:35.210 --> 00:13:37.009
would often have to put their feet up

00:13:37.009 --> 00:13:38.530
on the front of the chariot

00:13:38.530 --> 00:13:40.860
to be able to pull back
with all their might

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:43.007
to be able to get those horses

00:13:43.007 --> 00:13:44.300
to slow down in front of them.

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:46.630
And no CGI, no green screen.

00:13:46.630 --> 00:13:50.163
That is definitely a 10 for
a chariot race in my book.

00:13:55.650 --> 00:13:58.410
If you have a good riding
ability and a good horse,

00:13:58.410 --> 00:14:02.430
you can do anything on top of that horse.

00:14:02.430 --> 00:14:06.323
How the rider steps off in a flying Y

00:14:06.323 --> 00:14:09.120
or switches around for a
W, 'cause he comes back up

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:11.730
around the other way and
sits back up on that horse,

00:14:11.730 --> 00:14:14.360
once you form that
partnership with the horse,

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:18.101
again, you can do
anything with that horse.

00:14:23.140 --> 00:14:24.980
Cool. I wish I was that good.

00:14:24.980 --> 00:14:26.891
A lot of trick riding there.

00:14:26.891 --> 00:14:31.780
The bow that they have
is a mounted archery bow.

00:14:31.780 --> 00:14:33.840
They use thumb release, which is, again,

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:37.330
another tactic for mounted archery.

00:14:37.330 --> 00:14:41.510
So, historically, a Mongolian
archer is not somebody

00:14:41.510 --> 00:14:43.220
you want to meet in a back alley.

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:44.220
Absolutely not.

00:14:44.220 --> 00:14:47.020
So, yes, it is difficult,
but you have to realize

00:14:47.020 --> 00:14:49.600
that they've been riding since birth.

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:50.880
I think it's a great scene,

00:14:50.880 --> 00:14:52.890
but I'm not going to say it can't be done.

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:54.085
Absolutely it can.

00:14:54.085 --> 00:14:56.123
I'd give it a 9.

00:14:56.123 --> 00:14:57.913
Let them go!

00:15:02.420 --> 00:15:07.419
The style that they're
doing is open field,

00:15:07.419 --> 00:15:09.560
meaning that there's no actual list.

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:11.750
They stopped having open-field jousts

00:15:11.750 --> 00:15:14.930
because the horses were
colliding into one another

00:15:14.930 --> 00:15:17.050
and they didn't, again,
want those injuries

00:15:17.050 --> 00:15:19.018
happening to the horses.

00:15:19.018 --> 00:15:21.206
These were definitely war horses.

00:15:21.206 --> 00:15:24.820
To have two horses charging at one another

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:27.840
is something a normal
horse just does not do.

00:15:27.840 --> 00:15:30.730
You know, they are
fleeing, flight animals.

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:33.450
They are not turn-and-charge-down animals

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:36.290
unless it's a mare chasing off a stallion

00:15:36.290 --> 00:15:39.431
or another stallion fighting for turf

00:15:39.431 --> 00:15:40.538
with another stallion.

00:15:45.040 --> 00:15:48.510
The lances, they could
have done a better job.

00:15:48.510 --> 00:15:51.000
The lances, you could
tell, have a tip on them.

00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:53.060
So they were a tournament lance.

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:57.820
It's a cost-saving factor,
and they put new tips on.

00:15:57.820 --> 00:16:00.920
An actual joust of war
would've been a solid shaft

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:03.320
and would've had the pointed coronels.

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:06.560
Like, not the blunted coronel
or the flared coronel.

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:09.350
They would've had points on them.

00:16:09.350 --> 00:16:13.740
I'm not sure who was
teaching them how to joust,

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:17.832
but punching with your lance like that,

00:16:17.832 --> 00:16:19.539
not good. You want to hold that lance

00:16:19.539 --> 00:16:20.913
as tight as you can to your body,

00:16:20.913 --> 00:16:23.829
'cause that's what's going
to give that strength.

00:16:23.829 --> 00:16:26.610
You're not going to get
anything by throwing a punch

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:29.010
except for a broken
wrist or a broken hand.

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:37.450
So, when he ducked down,
the lance also tipped down

00:16:37.450 --> 00:16:39.740
and unfortunately killed that horse.

00:16:39.740 --> 00:16:42.767
This is the chest piece for a horse.

00:16:42.767 --> 00:16:44.010
The poitrel is what it's called.

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:45.290
Now, again, it would, you know,

00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:47.770
there to protect the front
of the horse's chest.

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:48.980
It still gives enough room

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:51.160
for the horse to move its legs underneath.

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:55.173
But behind this plate,
ultimately, is the horse's heart,

00:16:55.173 --> 00:16:56.130
and that's where the heart is,

00:16:56.130 --> 00:16:58.840
and one of the vulnerable spots

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:03.130
in warfare to strike a horse.

00:17:03.130 --> 00:17:05.440
And that's why they protected it immensely

00:17:05.440 --> 00:17:06.811
with these plates.

00:17:10.070 --> 00:17:11.970
This is a greave,

00:17:11.970 --> 00:17:15.103
and the thigh piece is a cuisse.

00:17:15.103 --> 00:17:17.520
Back in that time, the
French had the best armor.

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:19.320
So it kind of carried through

00:17:19.320 --> 00:17:21.700
in today's modern-day translation as well.

00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:25.010
This piece right here, this is, you know,

00:17:25.010 --> 00:17:28.220
would go on my left side, my bottom leg.

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:31.260
But you notice there's
nothing on the inside.

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:33.260
Well, there's nothing on
the inside of my calf,

00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:36.760
because that's how I
control and feel my horse.

00:17:36.760 --> 00:17:39.610
And the reason this is open is so my calf,

00:17:39.610 --> 00:17:42.562
the inside of my leg, can
feel the barrel of my horse

00:17:42.562 --> 00:17:44.330
and be able to give cues,

00:17:44.330 --> 00:17:46.500
whether it be left,
right, forward, or back.

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:49.530
Man, if they could have
not punched with the lances

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:52.910
and had a solid wooden lance,

00:17:52.910 --> 00:17:54.420
it would've been a definite 10.

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:57.440
But I'm going to have to give it a 9.

00:17:57.440 --> 00:18:00.973
If you enjoyed this video, click
the link above for another.

